    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is coherent, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase.\n\n- **Owner model**: The `deleted` field with `getDeleted`/`setDeleted` follows the existing Boolean-wrapper accessor pattern (matching `possibleDuplicate`), and the extra null-safe `isDeleted()` helper is justified \u2014 it's consumed by three call sites that all need a null-tolerant read, so the convenience method lives in the right unit. Field default `Boolean.FALSE` matches the \"newly created owner has false\" requirement without scattering that concern into callers.\n- **The three guards** (`CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique`, `CountOwnerNamesakes`, `DetectOwnerPossibleDuplicate`) each add a single `if (existing.isDeleted()) continue;` right beside the pre-existing self-skip guard in the same `findAll()` loop. This mirrors the established shape of these loops exactly rather than inventing a new abstraction. The one-line repetition is consistent with the already-repeated self-skip and doesn't warrant extraction \u2014 a shared helper would be out of step with the surrounding code.\n- **`DeleteOwner`** keeps its name (correct per the `Delete<E>` convention, since it still owns the delete semantics) and cleanly swaps `delete` for flag-and-save; the class Javadoc documents the new behavior in the natural place.\n- **Schema + OpenAPI**: the column is added uniformly across all four DB dialects and the `deleted` property is documented as `readOnly`, consistent with sibling derived/managed fields.\n\nLogic sits in the units a maintainer would expect, nothing has become a catch-all, and no duplication beyond what the existing style already accepts.",
